# KTP camera OCR — supervised pilot gate

Sprint doc: `docs/sprints/phase-1-patient-ktp-camera-ocr-supervised-pilot.md`.
Protocol: `docs/runbooks/ktp-camera-ocr-supervised-pilot-protocol.md`.
Extends rule 177 (OCR is a suggestion) — this rule decides WHO may get one.

1. **Two gates, both required.** The global flag `patient.ktp_camera_ocr` AND
   `KtpCameraOcrPilotGate` eligibility. The flag alone enables NOBODY.
2. **One decider.** `App\Modules\Patient\Services\KtpCameraOcrPilotGate` is the
   only reader of `config/patient_ktp_ocr_pilot.php`. The parse endpoint and the
   Blade camera UI both call it; neither re-derives any part. Hiding the camera
   button is not the boundary — the parse endpoint answers 404 for the same verdict.
3. **No "everyone" mode exists.** An empty or invalid cohort covers nobody.
   Widening beyond the pilot is a separate owner decision AND a code change —
   never an environment edit.
4. **Branch is server-resolved.** Eligibility uses
   `RmeWorkingBranchScope::activeBranchId()` (the selected working branch of a
   context-bound operator). A request `branch_id`, a tablet's branch or
   `users.branch_id` are never the authority. A governance role spanning every
   branch is ambiguous for a one-branch pilot and is refused.
5. **Exactly one branch, never MAIN**, active and RME-enabled; ceilings
   (`max_operators` 5, `max_branches` 1, `max_devices` 3, `max_period_days` 31)
   are committed in the config file and never read from the environment.
6. **Pilot period is inclusive, on the clinical calendar** (`ClinicalClock`,
   Asia/Makassar), strict `Y-m-d` round-trip — a rolled-over date is invalid.
7. **Device requirement fails safe ON.** Unset/blank/misspelled = required.
   `env()` turns the string "false" into boolean false — check the boolean
   BEFORE any string cast or an explicit owner "false" reads as unset.
8. **A device is bound only by the front-office trusted-device lock**, and the
   gate has NO device definition of its own: it requires
   `FrontOfficeBranchDeviceLockService::evaluate()` to ALLOW the request
   (enforcement on, account in that cohort, device active + key-proven +
   enrolment verified, at the account's branch — re-read every request), then
   narrows to the pilot allowlist and branch. With that lock OFF a
   device-requiring pilot is `ARMED_UNREACHABLE`, and an already-bound session
   stops getting OCR. Waiving the device requirement is a recorded owner decision.
9. **The gate writes, logs and audits nothing of its own.** Resolving the
   working branch can lazily retire an EXPIRED online context (canonical
   `UserOnlineContextService` behaviour; it only ever narrows). `posture()` and
   the status command write nothing at all. Do not claim "zero writes" for the
   request path.
10. **Measure, never infer.** `php artisan patient:ktp-ocr-pilot-status --json`
    (read-only; `--strict` exits 2 when the flag is on but the scope is unusable).
    It has no `--arm`. Never use `tinker` on production to check posture.
11. **Code deployment is not feature activation.** Activation = environment
    values + config cache rebuilt as `daengtisiams`, done in a supervised window.
12. **A physical pilot result is never fabricated.** Real-KTP accuracy and tablet
    behaviour are MEASURED by operators under consent, or reported as
    `PENDING PHYSICAL EXECUTION`. Pilot evidence is aggregated and carries no
    full NIK, name, address or KTP image.
13. **KTP images and OCR text never go to an unapproved third-party service**
    (no external OCR, analytics, chart tool or AI model).
14. **Wider rollout needs clinical pilot evidence** and a separate explicit
    authorization; a readiness GO tag is not that authorization.
15. **A device waiver is scoped to this gate.** `REQUIRE_BOUND_DEVICE=false` skips
    the gate's device clause — its only reader of `front_office.branch_device_lock`
    — so it can never weaken that lock, doctor devices, sessions or WebAuthn. Do
    not "complete" a waived pilot by arming the front-office device lock or adding
    device ids. Record the waiver and its reason with the activation.
16. **`ARMED` describes configuration, not today's eligibility.** The verdict does
    not read the pilot period: after `ends_on` it still says `ARMED` while every
    decision is `outside_pilot_period`. Ask `--user=<id>` for eligibility, and at
    period end disarm the flag or record a fresh owner decision extending it.
    First activation: `docs/operations/ktp-camera-ocr-pilot-activation-spn4-2026-10-10.md`.
17. **A code release does not change the pilot.** REVISION-PATIENT-KTP-OCR-FIELD-BASED-ROI-1
    (rule 179) changed how the card is read, not who may read it: the gate, its
    scope keys and the device waiver are untouched. While the pilot is armed, a
    deploy changes the read the pilot operator sees on their next scan; record
    that in the pilot evidence rather than re-arming or extending anything.
